INT. SURVEILLANCE VAN - NIGHT

Cramped. Monitors. Cold coffee in paper cups. A rain-streaked windshield looks out on a street of shuttered shops. Across the road, a dry cleaner's sign glows pink: DAN'S EXPRESS.

DET. COLM BRADSHAW, 50s, rumpled coat, eats a gas-station sandwich. OFFICER JUNE ALVAREZ-PARK, 20s, headphones around her neck, monitors a camera feed.

COLM
Anything?

JUNE
A cat.

COLM
That counts?

JUNE
It's more than we had at midnight.

He chews. Looks at the clock. 2:07.

COLM
You eaten?

JUNE
Granola bar.

COLM
That's not eating.

JUNE
It's got nuts.

COLM
(shrugging)
Fair.

Silence. Rain on the roof.

JUNE
Does it always take this long?

COLM
Always.

JUNE
How do you not go crazy?

COLM
I do. Slowly. Over thirty years.

She laughs. He sips cold coffee. Winces.

A knock on the side window. Both freeze. June drops a hand to her holster. Colm slides the window open an inch.

A WOMAN, 40s, umbrella, rain jacket, stands there holding a plastic bag.

WOMAN
Excuse me. Is this your van?

COLM
Can I help you, ma'am?

WOMAN
It's been parked here three nights. My husband says it's a stakeout.

June stares at Colm. Colm stares at the Woman.

COLM
We're from the gas company.

WOMAN
At two in the morning?

COLM
Emergency leak.

WOMAN
Smells fine to me.

COLM
That's the problem.

She lifts the bag.

WOMAN
I brought you sandwiches.

June blinks.

JUNE
Sandwiches?

WOMAN
Egg salad. You looked hungry.

Colm takes the bag. Peers in. Two wrapped sandwiches, a thermos.

COLM
Thank you.

WOMAN
I'm Felicity. I run the florist next door.

COLM
Colm.

FELICITY
Is it going to be a long night, Colm?

COLM
Could be.

FELICITY
Then I'll bring tea at four.

She turns. Walks off, umbrella bobbing, and disappears into the rain.

June looks at the sandwiches.

JUNE
She made us.

COLM
Of course she did. It's a florist.

JUNE
Do we move the van?

COLM
No.

JUNE
Why not?

COLM
Because she's bringing tea.

June laughs. Unwraps a sandwich. Takes a bite. Passes the other one to Colm.

On the monitor, a light goes on in the dry cleaner's back window. They both look up. The sandwich hovers.

JUNE
(sighing)
Four a.m.

COLM
Four a.m.

June eats. Colm adjusts the zoom. Rain falls. The sign glows. Nothing happens.

JUNE
She's right about the smell, though.

COLM
What smell?

JUNE
Egg salad.

COLM
It's a stakeout. Open a window.

She cracks the window. Cold wet air fills the van. Colm shuts it.

COLM (CONT'D)
Not that much.
